\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces We want to see how well the simulation matches experimental results. The simulation shows higher characteristic frequency of oscillation than experiment. There is clear chaotic dynamics going on because there is one positive, one near zero and one negative Lyapunov exponent. The experimental Lyapunov exponent weakly matches the non-negative theoretical component, approximately a 40\% disagreement, in the same range as the error found in calculating residuals.}}{9}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:voltage_vs_time}{{7}{9}{We want to see how well the simulation matches experimental results. The simulation shows higher characteristic frequency of oscillation than experiment. There is clear chaotic dynamics going on because there is one positive, one near zero and one negative Lyapunov exponent. The experimental Lyapunov exponent weakly matches the non-negative theoretical component, approximately a 40\% disagreement, in the same range as the error found in calculating residuals}{figure.caption.24}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(a)}{\ignorespaces {Theoretical simulation of $x$ versus time for 0 to 10 seconds. The time step in the RK4 algorithm is 0.125 ms.}}}{9}{subfigure.7.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(b)}{\ignorespaces {Experimental measurement of $x$ versus time for 0 to 10 seconds. There were 2001 samples, therefore the time step was 5 ms.}}}{9}{subfigure.7.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(c)}{\ignorespaces {Comparison of the two plots, despite its chaotic behaviour, we can see the theoretical simulation traces out the experimental results somewhat for 5 to 10 seconds. The residuals were calculated to be 42\%.}}}{9}{subfigure.7.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The top plot is the theoretical simulation of the bifurcation plot. We find a smooth curve with clear regions of nonchaos and chaos. The bottom plot is the bifurcation plot of experimental results. We find strange artefacts in measured peaks. These weird artefacts tend to occur at bifurcation plots. There is also another bifurcation point at 145 k$\Omega $ in the experimental plot, which is a strange artefact rather than reflective of the underlying physics. There also is a saturated region in the top branch of the experimental bifurcation plot at 135 k$\Omega $.}}{10}{figure.caption.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bicuration_plot}{{8}{10}{The top plot is the theoretical simulation of the bifurcation plot. We find a smooth curve with clear regions of nonchaos and chaos. The bottom plot is the bifurcation plot of experimental results. We find strange artefacts in measured peaks. These weird artefacts tend to occur at bifurcation plots. There is also another bifurcation point at 145 k$\Omega $ in the experimental plot, which is a strange artefact rather than reflective of the underlying physics. There also is a saturated region in the top branch of the experimental bifurcation plot at 135 k$\Omega $}{figure.caption.25}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(a)}{\ignorespaces {Bifurcation points at 52 k$\Omega $, 65 k$\Omega $, 68 k$\Omega $, 103 k$\Omega $, 125 k$\Omega $.}}}{10}{subfigure.8.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(b)}{\ignorespaces {Bifurcation points, approximately, at 52 k$\Omega $, 64 k$\Omega $, 68 k$\Omega $, 100 k$\Omega $, 113 k$\Omega $ and 118 k$\Omega $.}}}{10}{subfigure.8.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Calculated Lyapunov using the Wolf algorithm for theoretically simulated results. We see in regions where there are 2 negative exponents and 1 zero exponent, we have a limit cycle attractor. In regions of chaos, it can be defined as the regions where there are 1 positive, 1 zero and 1 negative exponents.}}{10}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lyapunov}{{9}{10}{Calculated Lyapunov using the Wolf algorithm for theoretically simulated results. We see in regions where there are 2 negative exponents and 1 zero exponent, we have a limit cycle attractor. In regions of chaos, it can be defined as the regions where there are 1 positive, 1 zero and 1 negative exponents}{figure.caption.26}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces We find that all plots except the plot for $Rv=78$ k$\Omega $ agree between experimental and theoretical simulated data. The experimental result for the chaotic attractor, $Rv = 95$ k$\Omega $ displays more chaos than theoretically expected. We can tell as the states fills outs most of the circular shape of the attractor whereas in the simulated result, there is some empty space within the circular attractor.}}{11}{figure.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:phase_space}{{10}{11}{We find that all plots except the plot for $Rv=78$ k$\Omega $ agree between experimental and theoretical simulated data. The experimental result for the chaotic attractor, $Rv = 95$ k$\Omega $ displays more chaos than theoretically expected. We can tell as the states fills outs most of the circular shape of the attractor whereas in the simulated result, there is some empty space within the circular attractor}{figure.caption.27}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(a)}{\ignorespaces {Theoretically simulated plots for variable resistance set to 53, 78, 95 and 128 k$\Omega $. A clear limit cycle attractor is shown in the top left and bottom right plots, indicating regions of nonchaos. The orbit appears to cross however this is a 2D cross section of a 3D plot and so the loop never intersects with itself in space. The chaotic strange attractors are shown in the top right and bottom left plot. We find there are trajectories that come really close to each other, however they never touch. In fact, they get infinitely close to each other but will never touch, this is fractal behaviour.}}}{11}{subfigure.10.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(b)}{\ignorespaces {Rather than plotting experimental data as lines, we choose to create a scatter plot of trajectories. This is because, our time step, experimentally, was too high to accurately resolve trajectories. Creating a scatter plot allows us to show the generic shape of the attractor. We can see all panels except the bottom left plot exhibit stable orbital behaviour. The bottom left plot clearly shows chaotic behaviour with no clear circular trajectory or shape.}}}{11}{subfigure.10.2}\protected@file@percent }
