\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Poincare sections at $x=0$, coming from positive $\dot  {x}$. The shape of the plane is different between simulated and experimental results. Theory predicts that the plane of intersection is a straight diagonal line, however the experimental data demonstrates that the plane is more of a concave up arc. The behaviour of Poincare sections for each Rv are the same across theory and experiment. We find that in regions of nonchaos, there are clear defined regions, exhibited by dark blue regions indicating high density of states. As Rv reaches regions of chaos, the density of states decreases and more spread out across the plot. In the theoretical Poincare section, we find a second layer of the plane, indicating small increased dimension of a 2D plane.}}{13}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:poincare}{{12}{13}{Poincare sections at $x=0$, coming from positive $\dot {x}$. The shape of the plane is different between simulated and experimental results. Theory predicts that the plane of intersection is a straight diagonal line, however the experimental data demonstrates that the plane is more of a concave up arc. The behaviour of Poincare sections for each Rv are the same across theory and experiment. We find that in regions of nonchaos, there are clear defined regions, exhibited by dark blue regions indicating high density of states. As Rv reaches regions of chaos, the density of states decreases and more spread out across the plot. In the theoretical Poincare section, we find a second layer of the plane, indicating small increased dimension of a 2D plane}{figure.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(a)}{\ignorespaces {Simulated result}}}{13}{subfigure.12.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline{(b)}{\ignorespaces {Experimental data}}}{13}{subfigure.12.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Fourier space plots for experimental data. In regions of nonchaos, there are narrow band signals, clearly indicating periodicity. For the spectrum in $Rv=95$, there is supposedly noise, however we know it is not noise as we do not see it in the other plots.}}{13}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fftcompare}{{13}{13}{Fourier space plots for experimental data. In regions of nonchaos, there are narrow band signals, clearly indicating periodicity. For the spectrum in $Rv=95$, there is supposedly noise, however we know it is not noise as we do not see it in the other plots}{figure.caption.31}{}}
